  "question": {
    "text": "<p>For a certain reaction at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>300</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ΔG</mi><mo>°</mo></math> for the same reaction is – ________ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math> (Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>314</mn><msup><mi>JK</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>)</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "57",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The standard Gibbs free energy of the formation of a compound is basically the change of Gibbs free energy that is followed by the formation of one  mole of that substance from its component element available at their standard states or the most stable form of the element which is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo> </mo><mo>°</mo></math>C and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn></math> kPa. Its symbol is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mo>˚</mo></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ΔG</mi><mo>°</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>RTℓnK</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>314</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>300</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5744</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mole</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>57</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>44</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mole</mi></math></p>"
  }
}